{
  "concept": "In a parallelogram built from u and v, the two diagonals are the resultants u + v and u − v, so subtracting a vector is the same as adding its reverse.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Left parallelogram: dark navy outline, lower-left origin point, horizontal bottom edge, slanted right edge rising to upper right",
    "Left parallelogram both diagonals drawn as thin navy lines (lower-left to upper-right, and upper-left to lower-right)",
    "Left: orange arrowhead on bottom edge pointing right, italic label 'u' below it",
    "Left: orange arrowhead on slanted right edge pointing up-and-right, italic label 'v' beside it",
    "Left: orange arrowhead at the upper-right end of the main diagonal, italic label 'u + v' along the diagonal",
    "Right parallelogram: identical navy outline and both thin diagonals",
    "Right: orange arrowhead on top edge pointing right, italic label 'u' above it",
    "Right: orange arrowhead on slanted right edge pointing down-and-right, italic label '−v' beside it",
    "Right: italic label 'u' below the bottom edge (translated copy of u)",
    "Right: descending diagonal from upper-left toward lower-right with orange arrowhead, italic label 'u − v'",
    "Shared tail vertex marked at lower-left of each figure",
    "Ghosted dashed copy of v (when reversed) to show −v is v flipped",
    "Legend/caption strip: orange = active vectors and resultant, navy = parallelogram frame"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "operation",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Operation",
      "options": ["u + v", "u − v"],
      "default": "u + v",
      "teaches": "Reversing v to −v swaps which diagonal of the very same parallelogram is the resultant, so subtraction is addition of the opposite vector."
    },
    {
      "id": "constructionStep",
      "type": "button",
      "label": "Construction step",
      "states": ["draw u", "attach v", "complete parallelogram", "reveal resultant"],
      "default": "draw u",
      "teaches": "The resultant is the end product of a head-to-tail construction: place u, attach v (or −v) at its head, close the parallelogram, and the diagonal from the common tail is the answer."
    },
    {
      "id": "selectDiagonal",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "Each diagonal belongs to a specific pair of edge vectors — clicking one lights up the two sides that generate it, showing u + v and u − v live in the same figure."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Start with u",
      "narration": "A single vector u is drawn from the origin along the bottom edge, orange arrowhead pointing right. Nothing else exists yet.",
      "state": { "operation": "u + v", "constructionStep": "draw u" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Attach v head to tail",
      "narration": "v is placed with its tail at the head of u, slanting up and to the right. The two vectors now form a bent path from the origin.",
      "state": { "operation": "u + v", "constructionStep": "attach v" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Close the parallelogram",
      "narration": "Copies of u and v complete the figure, so the same endpoint is reached by going u-then-v or v-then-u.",
      "state": { "operation": "u + v", "constructionStep": "complete parallelogram" }
    },
    {
      "title": "The sum is the long diagonal",
      "narration": "The diagonal from the common tail to the opposite corner is highlighted in orange and labeled u + v.",
      "state": { "operation": "u + v", "constructionStep": "reveal resultant" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Reverse v into −v",
      "narration": "Flipping the toggle turns v around: the arrowhead on the slanted edge now points down and to the right, labeled −v, while u is unchanged.",
      "state": { "operation": "u − v", "constructionStep": "attach v" }
    },
    {
      "title": "The difference is the other diagonal",
      "narration": "Head-to-tail with −v, the resultant becomes the descending diagonal labeled u − v — the same parallelogram, the other diagonal.",
      "state": { "operation": "u − v", "constructionStep": "reveal resultant" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "One parallelogram holds both answers: its two diagonals are u + v and u − v, because subtracting v is just adding the reversed vector −v.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Two congruent slanted parallelograms side by side on white, each outlined in thick dark navy (#1F4E79) with both diagonals drawn as thin navy lines. The shape leans right: horizontal bottom edge, right edge slanting up-right at roughly 60°. Active vectors and the active resultant are drawn in orange (#E8601C) with solid triangular arrowheads placed mid-edge or at the edge's end; inactive edges stay plain navy. Labels are bold italic serif letters (u, v, −v, u + v, u − v) set just outside their edge or alongside the diagonal. Left figure = addition: u on the bottom edge pointing right, v on the right edge pointing up-right, u + v along the lower-left-to-upper-right diagonal. Right figure = subtraction: u on the top edge pointing right, −v on the right edge pointing down-right, a second u label under the bottom edge, and u − v along the upper-left-to-lower-right diagonal. When the toggle animates, keep the outline fixed and only spin the v arrowhead 180° plus swap which diagonal is orange; a faint dashed ghost of the original v can remain to make the reversal visible. Clicking a diagonal thickens it and tints its two generating edges the same orange while dimming the rest.",
  "figureCaption": "Vector addition and subtraction as the two diagonals of one parallelogram."
}

